    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d322">

                <pb facs="0529" n="243" sameAs="frus:frus1916Supp/pg243"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72/2632</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Germany</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Gerard</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1916-04-25T11:00:00+01:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">April 25, 1916, 11 a. m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received April 27, 8.10 a.
                        m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>3805. Your 2927.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d322-f1"/> It occurs to me you might think
                    Von Jagow meant all ships. Although he used word “ships” only, he plainly meant
                    and I understood him to refer only to freight ships. Of course he was speaking
                    informally and indefinitely. In my conversations with Von Jagow we speak
                    English, a language he apparently speaks well, but says himself that his
                    vocabulary is limited. My last conversation with Chancellor was in German,
                    conversation before that in English, and sometimes Chancellor speaks French, and
                    sometimes he speaks German and I English. Of course I have to speak in language
                    he desires. I recall that Von Jagow also said that sinking merchant ships
                    without notice in war zone was their answer to England’s illegal blockade of
                    Germany.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gerard</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
